\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Covariance is inverse precision}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{Q} be a positive self-adjoint precision on a finite real
inner-product space #{(E,h)}, with its [Gaussian law](fdip-0005).
For #{j,k\in E}, put #{\ell_j(x)=h(j,x)}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\omega_Q(\ell_j)&=0,\\
\operatorname{Cov}_{\mu_Q}(\ell_j,\ell_k)
 &=h(j,Q^{-1}k).
\end{aligned}}
Thus its [covariance operator](fdip-002Z), relative to the specified
pairing, is #{C_Q=Q^{-1}}. On the counting vertex space,
#{\ell_{\delta_v}(\phi)=\phi(v)}, so
##{\omega_Q(\phi(v)\phi(w))
   =h_V(\delta_v,Q^{-1}\delta_w).}
For #{Q=L+m^2I}, this is the [Green kernel](fdip-000A), with no
vertex-volume factor. The density convention agrees with
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505, and Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical}
after substituting covariance #{Q^{-1}} for precision #{Q}.}
\proof{
\p{Write #{g_a(x)=\exp(-a x^2/2)} and
#{I(a)=\int_{\mathbb R}g_a(x)\,dx} for #{a>0}.
The [scalar normalization](fdip-002S) gives #{0<I(a)<\infty};
[finite polynomial moments](fdip-002W) give absolute integrability
of #{xg_a(x)} and #{x^2g_a(x)}. Reflection makes the first integral
its own negative, hence zero. On #{[-R,R]}, integrate
#{(xg_a(x))'=g_a(x)-a x^2g_a(x)} to obtain
##{\begin{aligned}
a\int_{-R}^R x^2g_a(x)\,dx
 &=\int_{-R}^R g_a(x)\,dx\\
 &\quad-2R\exp(-aR^2/2).
\end{aligned}}
For #{R\geq1}, #{e^{aR^2/2}\geq aR^2/2} gives
#{R e^{-aR^2/2}\leq 2/(aR)\to0}.
Monotone convergence on both nonnegative integrands, with their
already established finite integrals, therefore gives
##{I(a)^{-1}\int_{\mathbb R}x^2g_a(x)\,dx=a^{-1}.}}
\p{Choose an [orthonormal eigenbasis](fdip-002R) #{u_i} of #{Q}, with
#{Qu_i=q_i u_i}, #{q_i>0}. In #{x=\sum_i y_i u_i}, volume preservation
and [normalization](fdip-002T) give the product density
##{\prod_i g_{q_i}(y_i)/I(q_i).}
All coordinate products are absolutely integrable by \ref{fdip-002W},
so [signed Fubini](fdip-002Q) applies. Integrating the other coordinates
gives one. For #{i\ne j}, the two first moments give zero; for #{i=j},
the scalar second moment gives #{q_i^{-1}}. Thus
#{\omega_Q(y_i y_j)=\delta_{ij}q_i^{-1}} and every #{\omega_Q(y_i)=0}.
The finite expansion #{\ell_j(x)=\sum_i h(j,u_i)y_i} now yields
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(\ell_j,\ell_k)
 &=\sum_i q_i^{-1}h(j,u_i)h(k,u_i)\\
 &=h(j,Q^{-1}k).
\end{aligned}}
If two operators represented this bilinear form, their difference
applied to #{k} would be orthogonal to every #{j}, hence zero.
This proves uniqueness. In dimension zero the covariance is the zero
form, and its representing map is the unique invertible endomorphism.}
}
\p{For a nonorthonormal basis #{b_i}, the coordinate functional is
#{h(b^i,\cdot)}. The dual basis satisfies #{h(b^i,b_j)=\delta_{ij}}.
Its covariance array
therefore has entries #{h(b^i,Q^{-1}b^j)}; it is not the unqualified
array of the inverse operator in that basis. The counting basis is
orthonormal, giving the coordinate formula above. The
[alternating-cycle example](fdip-0037) evaluates it explicitly.}
\p{To compare with formal Gaussian providers, put #{n=\dim E}, choose an orthonormal
chart and let #{S} represent #{Q^{-1}} there. The positive inverse
is positive definite, #{S^{-1}} represents #{Q}, and
#{\det S=(\det Q)^{-1}}. Consequently the provider density is
##{\begin{aligned}
 &(2\pi)^{-n/2}(\det S)^{-1/2}
       e^{-y^TS^{-1}y/2}\\
 &\qquad=Z_Q^{-1}e^{-h(x,Qx)/2}.
\end{aligned}}
The [orthonormal chart](fdip-002L) and the Euclidean-space coordinate
wrapper preserve canonical volume. Thus the chart pushforward of
#{\mu_Q} is #{\mathrm{multivariateGaussian}(0,S)} by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.multivariateGaussian_eq_withDensity}.
Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.covariance_eval_multivariateGaussian}
then identifies its coordinate covariance with #{S}; its positive
semidefinite hypothesis follows from positive definiteness. The
parameter is covariance #{S=Q^{-1}}, not precision #{Q}. This density,
determinant and volume identification is needed in addition to the
covariance declaration; the integral proof above is independent of it.}
